       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f53ee0691ccb59ede3ec232c18af4e16?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FCW-5.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Chris Wilper",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=557058%3Ae25d94d0-bd50-4c2c-906c-14cdf85801fb",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p><span class=\"error\">&#91;Followup from Sourceforge Tracker&#93;</span><br/>\nFollowup Submitter: cwilper<br/>\nLogged In: YES <br/>\nuser_id=189298<br/>\nOriginator: YES</p>\n\n<p>Blair has submitted the necessary changes to PolicyEnforcementPoint.java and PolicyFinderModule.java</p>\n\n<p>On Feb. 5, 2007, Blair wrote:</p>\n\n<p>The major additions are in the PolicyFinderModule where the cache<br/>\noperations code lives.  The reason for changing the<br/>\nPolicyEnforcementPoint too is that PolicyFinderModule initialisation<br/>\nhad to be reworked to load the repository wide policies when the init<br/>\nmethod is called (this is done by sunxacml when the PDP is created).<br/>\nBecause the init method is declared in the sun code it couldn't throw<br/>\nan exception if loading of repo wide policies failed, thus I had to<br/>\nadd a check that is called after the PDP is constructed.</p>\n\n<p>There are several customisable parameters that might be worth exposing<br/>\nin fedora.fcfg:</p>\n<ul class=\"alternate\" type=\"square\">\n\t<li>the max cache sizes (that is the number of AbstractPolicies of each<br/>\ntype, Policy or PolicySet, that can be cached at any time), currently<br/>\n100 of each;</li>\n\t<li>the referenced policies directory, currently this is set to be<br/>\n\"referenced-policies\" at the same directory tree level as the<br/>\n\"repository-policies\" directory (currently if the directory does not<br/>\nexist it will NOT be created);</li>\n\t<li>the cache decay rate, this is the time in milliseconds it takes to<br/>\nreduce an inactive cache entry's weight by 1 (currently 1 minute).<br/>\nThe weight is used to select a row to replace on a cache miss when the<br/>\ncache is already full.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n<p>During testing I came across a rather strange behaviour which I think<br/>\nmay be a bug in the javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilder.  When a<br/>\nreference is included in a repo wide policy or another referenced<br/>\npolicy like so:<br/>\n&lt;PolicyIdReference&gt;<br/>\n   policy.xml<br/>\n&lt;/PolicyIdReference&gt;<br/>\nit works fine.  However when the same lines are included in a FOXML<br/>\nobject's POLICY datastream they cause an invalid URI exception on any<br/>\naccess attempt (making the object unreadable), this seems to be due to<br/>\nthe new line after the opening element.  Removing the formatting makes<br/>\nthe problem go away:<br/>\n&lt;PolicyIdReference&gt;policy.xml&lt;/PolicyIdReference&gt;</p>\n\n<p>Certainly the new line is invalid in a URI however it doesn't seem to<br/>\nbe passed along when read from a file, the only difference being which<br/>\nDocumentBuilder.parse method is called.  Just something to be aware of<br/>\nif/when idreference examples make it into the system documentation.</p>",
     "created": "07/Mar/07 11:51 PM",
